\section{A Conflict Graph Starts After Matching}

Blindell surveys two ways to translate selection over instantiated matches: independent sets in a conflict graph, and unate or binate covering \cite[chapter~4]{blindell2016instruction}. In the conflict graph there is one vertex per candidate, not per IR operation. Add an undirected edge if two matches cannot be selected together. With exact ownership, overlap of owned operation sets creates such an edge; merely reading the same input value does not. A pairwise prohibition, such as a particular consumer and an incompatible producer form, can also be an edge. A positive requirement that one of several producers be chosen takes more than an edge.

An independent set witnesses nonconflict, not necessarily coverage. A \emph{maximal} independent set cannot be extended by adding a vertex, whereas a \emph{maximum} one has the highest cardinality. Even a maximum set can leave a node uncovered: for instance, with footprints $\{a,b\}$ and $\{b,c\}$ and no other candidates, the maximum has size one and leaves either $a$ or $c$ uncovered. With a singleton for every required operation and edges only for overlap, a maximal set does cover the DAG: the singleton for any uncovered node could otherwise be added. Even then, maximizing cardinality may favor many singletons over one cheaper fused instruction.

Simply negating positive costs in an unconstrained maximum weighted independent set (MWIS) would make the empty set optimal. Suppose costs are nonnegative integers and conflicts prevent overlapping ownership. If every node is mandatory, use weight $K|F_j|-c_j$ for match $j$ with footprint $F_j$, where $K$ exceeds the sum of all candidate costs. Among independent sets, the summed footprint size is the number of nodes covered, so one more covered node outweighs any cost difference. Then the least-cost full cover wins if there is a full cover; check that it covers every required node. This objective does not repair missing operand or scheduling constraints.

\section{From Positive Rows to Signed Clauses}

In a unate covering matrix, each required operation is a row and each match is a column. An incidence entry of one means choosing that match covers that operation. With a Boolean variable for each column, a row is a disjunction requiring at least one of its columns. Unlike a conflict graph, the rows enforce coverage but do not prevent overlaps. General minimum-cost unate covering is NP-hard; its budget decision version is NP-complete. Without a budget or other restrictions, feasibility of positive rows is just a check that none is empty, since all columns can be chosen.

Binate covering permits negative as well as positive literals. A conflict between $j,k$ is $\neg j\lor\neg k$; together with the coverage rows, all overlap conflicts give exact ownership. A consumer $j$ that requires at least one of the compatible definitions $k_1,k_2$ gives $\neg j\lor k_1\lor k_2$. Attach costs to selected variables and minimize their sum over satisfying assignments. General signed-clause feasibility is already SAT, even without a cost budget. Conversely, in the graph formulation the usual NP-complete decision question is whether the weight reaches a threshold; finding the maximum-weight set is the optimization problem.

The two encodings have different ways of making candidates disappear. Choosing one vertex deletes its neighbors from the candidate graph, but unless the solver tracks coverage or dependencies separately, it will not notice that a still-needed producer has no candidates left. In the clause model, setting a variable true can make a negative literal false. If all but one literal of a required clause have become false, the remaining literal is forced. This lets a sole surviving producer force a choice before the solver has assigned every candidate. An operation with no candidates produces an empty positive row and signals infeasibility immediately.

