\section{Choose a Bank for Two Nibble Results}

An IR packs nibble inputs $x,y$ into the byte $a=16x+y$, then returns $b=(a\gg4)\mathbin{\mathtt{xor}}1$ and $c=(a\mathbin{\&}15)+1$ as bytes. Match $S$ packs into a scalar bank at cost one and match $V$ packs into a vector bank at cost four. Singleton consumers $B,C$ accept either bank and cost three and two respectively. The dual-output match $T$ computes both consumers at cost one, but only from a vector-bank input. There are no conversions or further candidates.

\Cref{fig:visual-273} branches on the dual-output match to show what the signed clauses force.

