\caption{Branching on \(T\) exposes why \(S,T\) is not a legal cost-two cover: the dependency \(T\Rightarrow V\) forces the vector producer.}
\label{fig:visual-273}
\end{figure}

\begin{verbatim}
coverage: (S or V), (B or T), (C or T)
conflicts: !S or !V; !B or !T; !C or !T
dependency: !T or V    # T needs the vector producer.

ignore dependency: S,T       costs 1+1 = 2   (illegal)
T false:           S,B,C     costs 1+3+2 = 6
T true:            V,T       costs 4+1 = 5
input (5,9): packed 0x59 -> (4,10)
                            # One T supplies both outputs.
\end{verbatim}

If $T$ is false, both singletons are forced and the scalar pack is cheaper. If $T$ is true, the implication forces the vector pack and the conflicts exclude both consumer singletons. These are the only two cases, so five is optimal. The positive rows alone cannot tell that the cost-two set is wrong; neither can a graph with only overlap edges. Here there are exactly two mutually exclusive producers, so adding an edge between $T$ and $S$, together with the pack coverage row, has the same effect as the implication. The implication generalizes directly when multiple producers or conversions could satisfy the input. Blindell's account of Liao and colleagues separates instruction selection from data transfers to limit clause growth. The trade-off is visible here: if the cheap scalar choice were frozen in an earlier pass, later repair could miss the jointly better vector cover.
